problems.problem:
A finite number of cells of an infinite square grid are coloured black. Prove that in this plane one can choose finitely many squares satisfying each of the following conditions:
(i) the interiors of any two different squares are disjoint (have empty intersection);
(ii) every black cell lies in one of these squares;
(iii) the area of the black cells in each of the chosen squares is at least $\frac{1}{5}$ and at most $\frac{4}{5}$ of the area of that square.
